  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Similar conditions",
   "text": "MPS IIIC shares biology with MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, and MPS IIID, because all of them involve the same kind of heparan sulfate storage, meaning the same sugar-like substance piles up inside cells [E7373ae4, Efdcc8cb, Ec586fdd, Eae4e3d8, E9f300a9]. MPS IIIC, MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, MPS IIID, and MPS II also share lysosomal enzyme deficiency, meaning each one is caused by a different missing or broken enzyme inside the lysosome [E108a4c3, E8282821, E8a7d8c7, Ef8f5811, Ef5d5a50]. MPS IIIC, MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, and MPS IIID all sit together in the same body pathway called \"Mucopolysaccharidoses\", which groups conditions involving broken-down sugar chains [Ed1d9a61, E472e813, Ef52839f, Ea3f8b18]. Children with MPS IIIC and MPS IIIA can both have a feature called dense calvaria, which means unusually thick skull bone [E4bf3fd5, E92597bb].",
