  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "What exists",
   "text": "Several family-run patient groups focus on MPS IIIC, including the Canadian Society for Mucopolysaccharide & Related Diseases Inc in Canada, AIMPS ETS in Italy, the Cure Sanfilippo Foundation in the United States, and Fundación Stop Sanfilippo in Spain [Ed13b8bf, E30302cc, E8303439, Ebc651dd]. A study called the \"Natural History Study of Participants With Sanfilippo Syndrome Type IIIC\" is recruiting, with sites in the United States and France, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E63413e3, Eba7dc9c, E20316bb]. Another study, \"Natural History to Assess Disease in Patients With MPS IIIC\", is planned but not yet recruiting, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E04abf2e, E569cb0a]. Two newborn screening programs, called ScreenPlus and Early Check, include MPS IIIC among the conditions they look for, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [E550e4bd, Ec294d25, E45546ef, E98a978e]. The funder NIH NINDS supports grants that study MPS IIIC, including one on manufacturing a gene therapy and one on finding biomarkers for MPS IIIC patients, according to NIH RePORTER [E76700d8, E0ddfd31, E21d5530, Edbe4076]. For the related condition MPS IIIA, a gene transfer trial is recruiting with sites in Australia, Spain, and the United States, according to ClinicalTrials.gov [Eebc4df2, E3023148, Eb3c0db6, E4164417, E5dbae4a].",
